    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Plain collagen peptides are hydrolysed protein with no meaningful carbohydrate, so they do not raise blood sugar.</strong> Several small trials found the opposite, reporting modest falls in fasting glucose, which is why the one thing to do before starting is tell the doctor managing your diabetes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It is protein, not sugar:</strong> Collabloom's label states 8,000mg of marine collagen giving 8g of protein and 35 kcal per 30ml shot. Protein alone accounts for roughly 32 of those calories, leaving almost no room for carbohydrate.</li>
        <li><strong>The trials point down, not up:</strong> two 2010 Chinese trials in 100 patients with type 2 diabetes reported better glucose and lipid markers on 13,000mg a day of marine collagen peptides (<em>Am J Med Sci</em> and <em>Appl Physiol Nutr Metab</em>).</li>
        <li><strong>The product is the risk, not the collagen:</strong> flavoured powders and gummies routinely carry added sugar or maltodextrin, a starch-derived carbohydrate that digests to glucose. Read the carbohydrate line, not the front of the pack.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> those glucose trials are small, single-centre, fifteen years old and used 13,000mg, well above Collabloom's 8,000mg. Collagen is not a treatment for diabetes and we do not sell it as one.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

