      <h2 id="raise">Does collagen raise blood sugar?</h2>
      <p>No. Collagen peptides are a hydrolysed protein, and protein is not sugar. A pure collagen peptide powder contains essentially no carbohydrate, so there is nothing in it to convert into glucose the way a biscuit or a glass of juice would.</p>
      <p>You can check this on any honest label with arithmetic rather than trust. Protein carries about 4 kcal per gram. Collabloom's panel states 8g of protein and 35 kcal per 30ml shot, so the protein alone accounts for around 32 kcal. Three calories is not enough space for a carbohydrate load that would matter to anyone's glucose reading.</p>
      <p>What can raise blood sugar is everything else a manufacturer puts in the tub to make collagen palatable. Collagen peptides taste faintly of broth, and the usual fix is sugar, fruit concentrate or maltodextrin. A gummy is the worst offender of the lot, because the gummy itself is a sugar confection with collagen mixed in.</p>
      <p>So the question "does collagen raise blood sugar" has the wrong subject. The collagen does not. The product around it very well might, and only the label will tell you which one you are holding.</p>

      <h2 id="lower">Can collagen lower blood sugar? What the trials found</h2>
      <p>A handful of small trials have reported that it does, modestly. This is genuinely what the published literature says, and it is also a finding we are going to spend the rest of this section arguing you should not rely on.</p>
      <p>The most cited work is a pair of 2010 papers by Zhu and colleagues from a single Chinese centre, studying the same cohort. The first, in the <em>American Journal of the Medical Sciences</em>, randomised 100 patients who had both type 2 diabetes and primary hypertension, alongside 50 healthy controls, to marine collagen peptides twice daily or to a patient control group for three months. The companion paper in <em>Applied Physiology, Nutrition, and Metabolism</em> reports the same cohort taking an additional 13g of marine collagen peptides daily, and concluded that the collagen peptides improved glucose and lipid metabolism. Two further small trials, one against resistant dextrin and one a dose-response study, have reported falls in fasting glucose; both were run by or with the peptide manufacturers.</p>
      <p>A 2024 systematic review and meta-analysis widened the lens to protein hydrolysates and bioactive peptides generally and found a small but statistically significant reduction in fasting blood glucose and HbA1c in the longer-term studies. The proposed mechanism across this literature is inhibition of DPP-4, the enzyme that breaks down the incretin hormones, which is the same target class as the gliptin drugs.</p>

