      <p>Now the reasons to hold all of that loosely. The glucose trials are small, single-centre, and most are fifteen years old without independent replication at scale. The ones with the largest reported effects were run by the companies selling the peptide. The meta-analysis pooled many peptide sources, not collagen alone, so it cannot tell you that collagen specifically did the work. And the dose that reported a benefit was 13,000mg a day, 63% more than the 8,000mg in <a href="/products/collabloom">Collabloom</a>. It is also worth knowing how this field behaves: a 2025 meta-analysis of 23 randomised collagen trials in <em>The American Journal of Medicine</em> found a pooled skin benefit overall but no effect in the studies that had not received pharmaceutical funding.</p>
      <p>Our position is the boring one. There is a signal, it is interesting, and it is nowhere near strong enough for anyone to take a collagen supplement as a way to manage their blood sugar. If your HbA1c needs to come down, the things that bring it down are the ones your doctor already named.</p>

