      <h2 id="safe">Is collagen safe if you have diabetes?</h2>
      <p>For most people with well-controlled diabetes, a plain collagen supplement with no added sugar is a protein supplement, and it is treated as one. Diabetes is not itself a reason to avoid collagen. The cautions that apply are mostly the ones that apply to everybody, which we cover in <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side effects</a>, plus two that are specific to you.</p>
      <p>The first is kidney function. Diabetes is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease, and collagen is a protein load. If you have diabetic kidney disease, or any reduction in eGFR, the amount of protein you take in is a question for the doctor managing it, not for a supplement label. An 8g protein shot is small next to a meal, but "small" is not a judgement a website can make for your kidneys.</p>
      <p>The second is the one almost nobody mentions, and it follows directly from the section above. If those trials are right and collagen peptides nudge fasting glucose down, then the people most affected are not the ones hoping for a benefit. They are the ones already on medication that lowers glucose.</p>

      <h2 id="medication">Can you take collagen with metformin or insulin?</h2>
      <p>Tell the doctor managing your diabetes before you start, and if you are on insulin or a sulfonylurea such as glimepiride, treat that as a firm instruction rather than a suggestion. There is no documented interaction between collagen peptides and any diabetes drug. The reason to speak up is different and simpler.</p>
      <p>Metformin on its own does not usually cause hypoglycaemia. Insulin and sulfonylureas do. If you add anything with even a modest glucose-lowering signal on top of a dose that is already tuned to your readings, the sensible response is to watch your numbers for a few weeks and let the person adjusting your medication know what you added. That is not a scary warning about collagen. It is the ordinary discipline of changing one variable at a time when a drug in your regimen can take your sugar too low.</p>
      <p>So: keep checking as you normally do for the first two to four weeks, and trust the reading rather than the supplement's reputation. If you take levothyroxine as well, which a great many Indian women with diabetes do, the spacing question is covered separately in <a href="/pages/collagen-and-thyroid-medication">collagen and thyroid medication</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="label">The five lines to check on the label</h2>
      <p>This is the part that actually changes an outcome. Formats differ far more than brands do, and the differences are printed on the pack.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Total carbohydrate, per serving.</strong> Not per 100g. A plain peptide powder should be at or near zero. Anything above a gram or two deserves an explanation.</li>
        <li><strong>Of which sugars.</strong> The line brands hope you skip. A flavoured powder can carry several grams; a gummy is a sugar sweet with collagen in it.</li>
        <li><strong>Maltodextrin, dextrose, glucose syrup, fruit juice concentrate, honey.</strong> All carbohydrate, none of them described as sugar on the front of the pack. Maltodextrin can also arrive hidden as a carrier inside "natural flavour".</li>
        <li><strong>The collagen figure in milligrams.</strong> If it is inside a proprietary blend you cannot know the dose, which makes the rest of the label unverifiable too. We go through the numbers in <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">how much collagen per day</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Calories per serving.</strong> The cross-check. Protein is about 4 kcal per gram, so if the calories are far above four times the protein grams, the difference is carbohydrate or fat.</li>
      </ol>

