      <h2 id="sweetener">Is the sweetener a problem?</h2>
      <p>For stevia, the trial evidence says no, with one caveat worth printing. Steviol glycosides are not metabolised to glucose, and the controlled studies have largely found no effect on glycaemic markers.</p>
      <p>A double-blind trial in 34 patients with type 2 diabetes compared stevia-sweetened and sucralose-sweetened tea and found no effect on blood glucose, HbA1c, insulin or lipids. A 12-week randomised trial in healthy adults found no significant difference in the glucose or insulin response to an oral glucose tolerance test, and a crossover study giving 200mg of stevia before a 75g glucose load found no difference in glucose or insulin area under the curve.</p>
      <p>The caveat: not every study agrees. One randomised trial in insulin-resistant women reported higher serum glucose at 30 and 60 minutes after a glucose load with a rebaudioside A beverage than with water. That is a single finding in one group, and the weight of the evidence sits the other way, but it is the reason to say "no meaningful effect" rather than "no effect".</p>
      <p>Collabloom is sweetened with stevia and carries no added sugar, at 35 kcal per 30ml shot. That is a reason it is a reasonable thing to take if your glucose matters, and it is not a claim that it helps your glucose.</p>

