      <h2 id="skin">What diabetes does to your skin</h2>
      <p>This is the part I see in clinic, and it is the reason a dermatologist ends up asking about glucose at all. Diabetes shows on skin, and skin sometimes shows diabetes before a blood test does.</p>
      <p>The clearest example is acanthosis nigricans: velvety, darkened, slightly thickened skin at the back of the neck, in the armpits, the groin or the knuckles. Patients almost always arrive describing it as dirt that will not wash off, or as pigmentation, and ask for a cream. It is neither. It is a cutaneous marker of insulin resistance, and in a UK case-control study of young overweight adults it was the strongest predictor of insulin resistance once BMI and other risk factors were in the model.</p>
      <p>Two honest limits on that. It is a specific but not a sensitive marker, so its absence rules nothing out, and the studies disagree on whether it works as a formal screening test. And insulin resistance is not its only cause: thyroid disease, Cushing's, PCOS and some drugs produce the same appearance. What it reliably is, is a reason to get a glucose test rather than a tube of cream.</p>
      <p>None of that is an argument for collagen, and I want to be plain about it. No trial has shown that an oral collagen supplement improves acanthosis nigricans, diabetic skin changes or wound healing. Collagen's skin evidence is about hydration and elasticity in healthy adults, a different question, and we lay out how good it actually is in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>. Acanthosis nigricans responds to the insulin resistance behind it, which means weight, activity and glycaemic control.</p>

      <h2 id="doctor">When to see a doctor instead</h2>
      <p>Diabetes changes which skin problems are urgent, because infection spreads faster and sensation can be reduced. Do not wait, and do not start a supplement instead, if any of these apply:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Any break in the skin on the foot, however small or painless, including a blister, crack between toes or callus that has darkened</li>
        <li>Spreading redness, warmth, swelling or a red streak moving up a limb</li>
        <li>A wound that is not healing, smells, or is leaking fluid or pus</li>
        <li>Numbness, burning or pins and needles in the feet, or a loss of feeling you had not noticed before</li>
        <li>Rapidly worsening pain, skin turning dusky or black, or fever with any skin infection</li>
        <li>New velvety darkening at the neck, armpits or knuckles, if your glucose has never been tested</li>
        <li>Repeated hypoglycaemia, or readings that have changed pattern since you started anything new</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A foot infection in diabetes is a same-day problem, not a next-week one. Pain that is severe and out of proportion to how the skin looks, especially with fever, needs emergency assessment rather than a clinic appointment.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen raise blood sugar?</summary>
          <p>No. Collagen peptides are hydrolysed protein and contain essentially no carbohydrate, so there is nothing in plain collagen to convert into glucose. What raises blood sugar is what a manufacturer adds for taste, such as sugar, fruit concentrate or maltodextrin. Check the carbohydrate line per serving.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen safe for diabetics?</summary>
          <p>For most people with well-controlled diabetes, a collagen supplement with no added sugar is treated like any other protein supplement. The two things to raise with your doctor are your kidney function, because diabetes is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease and collagen is a protein load, and any medication you take that lowers glucose.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen lower blood sugar or HbA1c?</summary>
          <p>A few small trials reported modest falls in fasting glucose and HbA1c, including two 2010 Chinese studies in 100 patients using 13,000mg a day. The trials are small, single-centre, mostly manufacturer-run and not independently replicated at scale. Collagen is not a treatment for diabetes and should not be taken as one.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen with metformin?</summary>
          <p>There is no documented interaction between collagen peptides and metformin, and metformin alone rarely causes hypoglycaemia. Tell the doctor managing your diabetes anyway, and monitor your readings for the first two to four weeks. If you are on insulin or a sulfonylurea such as glimepiride, speak to them before starting rather than after.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the stevia in a sugar-free collagen shot safe for diabetics?</summary>
          <p>The controlled evidence says it has no meaningful effect on glucose. A double-blind trial in 34 patients with type 2 diabetes found stevia-sweetened tea changed neither blood glucose, HbA1c, insulin nor lipids. One trial in insulin-resistant women did report higher glucose at 30 and 60 minutes after a glucose load, so the evidence is not unanimous.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How many calories and carbs are in a collagen shot?</summary>
          <p>Collabloom's label states 35 kcal and 8g of protein per 30ml shot, with no added sugar. Since protein carries about 4 kcal per gram, the 8g of protein accounts for roughly 32 of those 35 calories, which leaves almost no room for carbohydrate. Use that arithmetic on any label you are shown.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are collagen gummies a bad choice if I have diabetes?</summary>
          <p>They are the worst format for this purpose. A gummy is a sugar confection with collagen added, so it carries the highest carbohydrate load of any format while usually delivering a low collagen dose. An unflavoured powder or a sugar-free shot avoids both problems.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help diabetic wounds or skin heal?</summary>
          <p>No trial has shown that an oral collagen supplement improves wound healing or diabetic skin changes. Collagen dressings applied to a wound are a separate medical product and not the same thing as a drink. Any non-healing wound or foot break in diabetes needs a doctor the same day, not a supplement.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

